<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Final exam problems on the cross product
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-cross-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems use the cross product and the triple product. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rw23-9-a">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement correct="no">
<p>
If <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> are two non-zero vectors in
<m>\R^3</m>, then
<m>\vec u \cdot (\vec u \times \vec v)
= \|\vec u\|^2 + \vec u \cdot \vec v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<feedback>
<p>
Counterexample: take
<m>\vec u = \mathbf i</m> and <m>\vec v = \mathbf i</m>. Then
<md>
\mathbf i \cdot
\underbrace{(\mathbf i \times \mathbf i)}_{\vec 0} = 0,
\quad\text{whereas}\quad
\|\mathbf i\|^2 + \mathbf i \cdot \mathbf i = 1 + 1 = 2,
</md>
and <m>0 \ne 2</m>.
</p>
</feedback>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-8">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf v</m> be the vector
<m>3\,\mathbf i - 4\,\mathbf j + 5\,\mathbf k</m>.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find a non-zero vector <m>\mathbf v'</m> orthogonal to
<m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find a vector orthogonal to both <m>\mathbf v</m> and
<m>\mathbf v'</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
We need <m>\langle a,b,c\rangle</m> with
<m>3a - 4b + 5c = 0</m>. Any solution will do; taking <m>c = 0</m>
leaves <m>3a = 4b</m>, so <m>a = 4</m>, <m>b = 3</m> works:
<md>
\mathbf v' = 4\,\mathbf i + 3\,\mathbf j,
\qquad
\mathbf v \cdot \mathbf v' = 12 - 12 + 0 = 0.
</md>
(There are infinitely many correct answers.)
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The cross product is orthogonal to both:
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf v \times \mathbf v' \amp =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
3 \amp -4 \amp 5 \\
4 \amp 3 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (0 - 15)\mathbf i - (0 - 20)\mathbf j + (9 + 16)\mathbf k</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -15\,\mathbf i + 20\,\mathbf j + 25\,\mathbf k.</mrow>
</md>
Dividing by <m>5</m> gives the simpler answer
<m>-3\,\mathbf i + 4\,\mathbf j + 5\,\mathbf k</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-13">
<statement>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Find the area of the triangle with vertices <m>(0,1,1)</m>,
<m>(-1,2,4)</m>, <m>(3,0,1)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the equation of the plane containing the triangle.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Take <m>A(0,1,1)</m> as the base vertex and form two edge vectors:
<md>
\vec a = \vec{AB} = \langle -1, 1, 3\rangle,
\qquad
\vec b = \vec{AC} = \langle 3, -1, 0\rangle.
</md>
Their cross product is
<md>
\vec a \times \vec b =
\begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf i \amp \mathbf j \amp \mathbf k \\
-1 \amp 1 \amp 3 \\
3 \amp -1 \amp 0
\end{vmatrix}
= (0+3)\mathbf i - (0-9)\mathbf j + (1-3)\mathbf k
= \langle 3, 9, -2\rangle.
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The magnitude of the cross product is the area of the parallelogram
spanned by <m>\vec a</m> and <m>\vec b</m>, so the triangle has half
that area:
<md>
\text{Area} = \frac12\left|\vec a \times \vec b\right|
= \frac12\sqrt{9 + 81 + 4}
= \frac{\sqrt{94}}{2}.
</md>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The cross product is normal to the plane, so the plane has the form
<m>3x + 9y - 2z = D</m>. Substituting the point <m>(0,1,1)</m> gives
<m>D = 0 + 9 - 2 = 7</m>, hence
<md>
3x + 9y - 2z = 7.
</md>
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-4">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose <m>\|\vec A\| = 3</m>, <m>\|\vec B\| = 4</m> and
<m>\|\vec A\times\vec B\| = 6</m>. Find
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
<m>\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|</m>;
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B\right\|</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<term>(a)</term> Let <m>\theta</m> be the angle between the two
vectors, <m>0 \le \theta \le \pi</m>. From
<m>\|\vec A\times\vec B\| = \|\vec A\|\,\|\vec B\|\sin\theta</m>,
<md>
6 = (3)(4)\sin\theta \quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\sin\theta = \frac{6}{12} = \frac12 .
</md>
Since <m>\sin^2\theta + \cos^2\theta = 1</m>,
<md>
|\cos\theta| = \sqrt{1 - \tfrac14} = \frac{\sqrt3}{2} .
</md>
(We cannot determine the <em>sign</em> of <m>\cos\theta</m>, since
<m>\theta</m> could be <m>30^\circ</m> or <m>150^\circ</m>; this is
exactly why the problem asks for the absolute value.) Therefore
<md>
\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|
 = \|\vec A\|\,\|\vec B\|\,|\cos\theta|
 = (3)(4)\frac{\sqrt3}{2} = 6\sqrt3 .
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> The projection is
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B
= \left(\dfrac{\vec A\cdot\vec B}{\|\vec A\|^2}\right)\vec A</m>, so
its length is
<md>
\left\|\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B\right\|
 = \frac{\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|}{\|\vec A\|^2}\,\|\vec A\|
 = \frac{\left|\vec A\cdot\vec B\right|}{\|\vec A\|}
 = \frac{6\sqrt3}{3} = 2\sqrt3 .
</md>
As a sanity check, <m>2\sqrt3 \approx 3.46</m>, which is less than
<m>\|\vec B\| = 4</m>, as a shadow must be.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
